    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go a bit deeper into the cellular level though? Like, how does the cell actually \"sense\" that leucine levels are high enough to trigger that mTOR response? Is there a specific sensor involved?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The cellular \"sensing\" of leucine is quite complex, but it involves a dedicated mechanism that is less about a single, simple sensor and more about the availability of the amino acid itself. High intracellular leucine levels are thought to induce conformational changes or directly interact with components upstream of mTORC1, particularly within the Rag GTPases. These Rag GTPases are the critical molecular switch that senses the nutrient status of the cell, effectively telling the mTOR complex whether it has the necessary building blocks (like leucine) to initiate an anabolic response. If leucine levels drop, the Rag GTPases shift conformation, and the signal to mTORC1 shuts down, halting muscle building.",
      "source": "target",
