    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So it's basically a threshold mechanism. Following that logic, if my client is in a massive deficit or if they're missing certain amino acids, what does that do to the signaling? Does the whole pathway just stall out completely, or is there still some level of activity happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When your client is in a massive caloric deficit or is deficient in key amino acids, the mTOR pathway doesn't necessarily \"stall out\" completely, but its activity is severely dampened, which is a metabolic state often seen in a low-energy, high-stress environment. This is a survival mechanism, as the body prioritizes maintaining essential functions over building new muscle mass. The signaling becomes highly sensitive to the scarcity, meaning even small fluctuations in amino acid availability can cause a significant drop in mTOR activity.\n\nIt's not a binary on/off switch; rather, it's a dimmer switch that gets turned way down. The cell shifts into a catabolic or maintenance mode, reducing the rate of protein synthesis to conserve energy, which is precisely what you want to avoid when trying to build muscle. This is where the concept of nitrogen balance becomes critical—if the intake can't support the synthesis, the body starts cannibalizing its own muscle tissue to meet energy demands.",
      "source": "target",
